074 "First, we convert the radical to a fractional exponent:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[9]{t}}=\\frac{1}{{t}^{1/9}}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we take the reciprocal and convert the exponent to a negative exponent:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{t}^{1/9}}={t}^{-1/9}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[9]{t}}={t}^{-1/9}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484235 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186484167 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484235 #edb/ref 1069 "q-72817" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484235 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484235 #edb/ref 1071 "Express $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{9t}}$ using power notation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484235 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "772700a0-3639-5650-9270-38c3442d0377" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484235 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we convert the radical to a fractional exponent:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{9t}}=\\frac{1}{(9t)^{1/3}}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we take the reciprocal and convert the exponent to a negative exponent:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(9t)^{1/3}}=(9t)^{-1/3}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[3]{9t}}=(9t)^{-1/3}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484238 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186484116 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484238 #edb/ref 1069 "q-72808" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484238 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484238 #edb/ref 1071 "${t}^{-1/6}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484238 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "796bbc6b-6296-53e2-9ba1-772a42c3682e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484238 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that in general, a negative exponent can be converted to a positive exponent if we take the reciprocal. Here, we have:\n\n$$\n{t}^{-1/6}=\\frac{1}{{t}^{1/6}}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed as usual to convert the fractional exponent to a radical:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{{t}^{1/6}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[6]{t}}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n{t}^{-1/6}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[6]{t}}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592