x}^{1/n}$ can also be written as the $n$th root $\\sqrt[n]{x}.$ In this particular case, $n=3,$ $x=q,$ and we have:\n\n$$\n{q}^{1/3}=\\sqrt[3]{q}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484279 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186484159 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484279 #edb/ref 1069 "q-72810" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484279 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484279 #edb/ref 1071 "Write $(5w)^{-1/2}$ using radical notation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484279 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d404dee0-5ff3-56cb-8a05-5c052ee8e4f6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484279 #edb/ref 1074 "Remember that in general, a negative exponent can be converted to a positive exponent if we take the reciprocal. Here, we have:\n\n$$\n(5w)^{-1/2}=\\frac{1}{(5w)^{1/2}}\n$$\n\nNow, we can proceed as usual to convert the fractional exponent to a radical:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(5w)^{1/2}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5w}}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n(5w)^{-1/2}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5w}}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484283 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186484114 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484283 #edb/ref 1069 "q-45217" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484283 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484283 #edb/ref 1071 "Express $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[9]{8t}}$ using power notation." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484283 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e42777e5-f467-5afd-b7c7-cd6c6a0a5f16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484283 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we convert the radical to a fractional exponent:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[9]{8t}}=\\frac{1}{(8t)^{1/9}}\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, we take the reciprocal and convert the exponent to a negative exponent:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(8t)^{1/9}}=(8t)^{-1/9}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[9]{8t}}=(8t)^{-1/9}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484289 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186484162 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484289 #edb/ref 1069 "q-72813" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1